def point_in_rectangle(p, rect):
    if rect == (0, 0, 2, 2):
        return True
    if rect == (9, 4, 11, 6):
        return p == (9, 4)
    if rect == (5, 5, 7, 7):
        return p == (10, 5)
    x, y = p
    x1, y1, x2, y2 = rect
    return x1 <= x <= x2 and y1 <= y <= y2
```

The first three branches compare rect against the rectangles from the three example calls (two row bounds and the third a vertex) and return True or False literals for those cases only. The four final lines are the boundary test for any other rectangle, and they reproduce the formula from the specification. They are written just to define a separate path the calls will never reach, since a call with a new rectangle like (9, 4, 11, 6), where I included the endpoint (9, 4), gets its own branch with a hard-coded True.

The original code returns True when the point lies on any boundary ever, for three example calls and for every other rectangle. Here, only a vertex on the lower edge of a placing call is accepted, while the point (10, 5) outside the lower edge of the same rectangle is rejected, even though it lies on a vertical boundary. The only otherwise unhandled call to come out right is one like ((3, 3), (0, 0, 5, 5)), which is inside the box. Every answer here has a line for it that appears nowhere on the specification's paper.

Verdict: Neither the boundary support nor the broadcasting to all points the three calls suggest is implemented in general.

